---
scope: src/lib
intent: >
  This is the layer that makes the server's promises actionable. When a tool
  tells an agent "your project has no naming conflicts" or "your editor is now
  configured to preview .aide files", something below had to read or touch the
  project on disk and come back with an honest answer. The consumer — the tool
  above, and indirectly the agent the tool is speaking to — relies on this
  layer to be deterministic where it claims to be deterministic, to be honest
  about confidence where it is guessing, and to never damage a user's project
  in the process of helping them adopt the convention.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - A health check can distinguish a finding the server is certain about
      (two competing spec files in the same folder) from a finding the server
      is guessing at (this folder looks like an orchestrator that ought to
      have a spec) so that an agent acting on the report knows which items
      are mandatory and which require human judgement.
    - Running the same operation twice against the same project produces the
      same result on the second run as on the first — a second init adds
      nothing new, a second scaffold refuses to stomp on an existing spec, a
      second validate reports the same findings in the same categories.
    - Adopting AIDE never costs a user their existing editor or project
      configuration. After setup, anything the user had configured in
      `.zed/settings.json`, `.mcp.json`, or `CLAUDE.md` before the server
      touched those files is still there, unchanged, alongside the new AIDE
      entries.
    - Framework detection returns the same answer every time for the same
      project, so the `CLAUDE.md` a new team member generates today matches
      the one a teammate generated last week — the convention doesn't drift
      based on who ran init.
  undesired:
    - A validation report that labels a heuristic guess as an error. An agent
      that trusts an authoritative error will either fabricate a spec it
      doesn't understand, or — once it learns the error was soft — dismiss
      every future finding from the validator as noise. Both outcomes are
      worse than a clearly-labelled warning.
    - A user opens their project after running init and discovers their Zed
      theme, keybindings, or language settings are gone because setup
      replaced `.zed/settings.json` instead of merging into it. Silent data
      loss is the single fastest way to burn trust in the tool.
    - A report that looks right on the surface but depends on the order the
      project was walked, the host OS's path separator, or which folder the
      user ran the server from. Non-determinism here turns every subsequent
      tool response into a guess.
    - A validation pass that misses the failure modes the convention actually
      cares about — a folder containing both `.aide` and `intent.aide`, a
      `research.aide` with no intent alongside it, a `.aide` sitting in a
      folder with no orchestrator. These are the exact failures the
      convention exists to prevent; missing them makes the validator
      ornamental.
---

## Context

This is the only place in the server where real work happens — everything
above this layer is wiring. Because every tool's response is ultimately a
statement about the project on disk, any dishonesty here (a guess reported as
a fact, a write that clobbers instead of merges, a walk whose result depends
on host quirks) contaminates every tool at once. The cost of a mistake in
this layer is not "one tool gives a bad answer"; it is "the agent stops
trusting the server".

The users of this layer are not humans reading output — they are tool
handlers one layer up, and through them, agents acting on the results. Agents
retry aggressively when they're uncertain, and humans re-run init whenever
they set up a new machine or a new feature. Both modes of use are
first-class. Neither can be allowed to break the project.

## Strategy

**Certainty and heuristic must be visibly different in the output.** A
validation report mixes two kinds of findings: things the convention forbids
outright (a folder holding both `.aide` and `intent.aide`, a `research.aide`
sitting alone with no intent next to it) and things the convention suggests
but cannot prove (a folder that *looks* like an orchestrator because it has
several sibling folders it pulls from, but might just be a barrel). The first
kind is a hard failure; the second kind is a prompt for human review. The
report surfaces both, but it labels them differently — hard failures as
errors, heuristic findings as warnings. If the two were merged, an agent
would either treat every warning as blocking (and fabricate specs for modules
that don't need them) or learn that errors sometimes don't matter (and start
ignoring real errors). The split exists so agents can make the right call
automatically.

**Every write into a user-owned file merges, never replaces.** `.zed/settings.json`,
`.mcp.json`, and `CLAUDE.md` are files a user may already have invested hours
in. The rule is: read the current contents, add what AIDE needs, write back —
and if what AIDE needs is already present, write nothing at all. The
alternative ("just write the expected config") will eventually eat a user's
unrelated customization, and that single incident of data loss is more
expensive than every ounce of merge complexity combined. The cost of losing
trust once is permanent; the cost of writing a merge is paid in code review,
not in the user's confidence.

**Idempotency is the contract for every operation that touches disk.** Agents
retry tool calls when responses are ambiguous; users re-run init when they
reset an environment or add a new feature. Both flows must converge on the
same final state as a single clean run. "Running it twice is dangerous"
cannot be a footnote on any tool this server exposes — the tools are agent
surfaces, and an agent that has to remember which tools are safe to retry has
already lost.

**Anomaly detection reports only the failure modes the convention actually
cares about, and labels each finding honestly.** The convention forbids four
things: a folder holding both `.aide` and `intent.aide` (naming conflict),
a `research.aide` with no intent alongside (orphaned research), a `.aide` or
`intent.aide` with no orchestrator in the folder (orphaned spec), and an
orchestrator folder with real domain logic that has no spec (missing spec).
The first two are deterministic from the file list alone. The last two are
heuristic — "orchestrator with domain logic" is a guess, and the report must
say so. Any detector for a failure mode outside this list is scope creep;
any detector that reports a guess as a certainty is lying.

**Determinism is a property of every read path.** Two runs against the same
project must produce the same discovery tree, the same framework detection,
the same validation report, in the same order. Walks are sorted; filesystem
order is never trusted. Framework detection is a function of what the
project contains, never of when or where the server runs. Non-determinism
here would mean a `CLAUDE.md` generated today differs from one generated
yesterday on the same project, and the convention would drift into
"whatever the last person ran produced".

## Good examples

A developer runs a health check on a project that has a clean spec layout
except for one folder containing both `.aide` and `intent.aide`, and one
service module with four sibling helper folders and no spec. The report:

```
2 findings

ERRORS (1)
  src/service/billing
    naming conflict: folder contains both `.aide` and `intent.aide`
    resolution: keep one, delete the other

WARNINGS (1)
  src/service/scoring
    heuristic: this folder looks like an orchestrator (4 sibling folders
    imported by its index) but has no spec
    resolution: either add a spec or confirm this orchestrator is simple
    enough not to warrant one
```

The agent reading this report knows the first item must be fixed; the second
item is a prompt for a human decision. Both are actionable; neither is
misleading.

A user who already has a customized `.zed/settings.json` runs init for the
first time. Their file before:

```json
{
  "theme": "Gruvbox Dark",
  "vim_mode": true,
  "language_overrides": {
    "TypeScript": { "tab_size": 2 }
  }
}
```

Their file after:

```json
{
  "theme": "Gruvbox Dark",
  "vim_mode": true,
  "language_overrides": {
    "TypeScript": { "tab_size": 2 }
  },
  "file_types": {
    "Markdown": ["*.aide"]
  }
}
```

Running init a second time produces the same file, byte for byte. The
customizations are intact. The new entry is present. No surprise.

A teammate runs framework detection on a Next.js project on Monday and gets
back `nextjs`. Another teammate runs it on the same commit on Friday from a
different machine and gets back `nextjs`. The resulting `CLAUDE.md` content
is identical on both machines. No team member has to wonder whose copy is
"the real one".

## Bad examples

A validation report that looks like this:

```
ERRORS (2)
  src/service/billing
    naming conflict: folder contains both `.aide` and `intent.aide`
  src/service/scoring
    missing spec: orchestrator has no `.aide`
```

The second finding is a heuristic — the validator guessed that `scoring` is
an orchestrator by counting its sibling imports. Labelling that guess as an
error tells the agent the validator is authoritative about it. The agent now
either writes a fabricated spec without understanding why, or, once it
discovers the error was soft, learns to treat all future errors from this
server as unreliable. Both failure modes poison the feedback loop the
convention depends on.

A user's `.zed/settings.json` before init:

```json
{
  "theme": "Gruvbox Dark",
  "vim_mode": true,
  "language_overrides": {
    "TypeScript": { "tab_size": 2 }
  }
}
```

After init:

```json
{
  "file_types": {
    "Markdown": ["*.aide"]
  }
}
```

Technically, running init again produces the same file. Technically, the new
entry is present. Practically, the user's theme, vim mode, and language
override are gone, and the user blames AIDE the moment they reopen Zed. The
tool is idempotent and still catastrophically wrong.

A validator that reports an orphaned spec finding for a folder containing
`intent.aide` and a `research.aide`, because it was only checking for `.aide`
and didn't know `intent.aide` counts as a spec. The report is confident, the
rule it's enforcing is wrong, and the user deletes a valid spec on the
validator's advice. The convention's four failure modes are fixed — the
validator must cover all of them, and only them.

A discovery walk whose output changes between runs because the underlying
directory enumeration isn't sorted. On one run the tree shows
`billing` before `scoring`; on the next, `scoring` before `billing`. Nothing
about the project changed. The agent downstream now has to decide whether
order carries meaning, and any diff between two runs is noise it has to
filter. Determinism isn't a nice-to-have — it's the contract that lets the
agent trust every other answer.
